-- title:
--   The closed cube as a product of coordinate intervals
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ with $h \ge 0$, and let $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Write $\bar A_h(u)$ for the closed cube of side $h$ centred at $u$. Then $\bar A_h(u)$ is exactly the set of points whose coordinates all lie within $h/2$ of those of $u$:
--
--   $$\bar A_h(u) \;=\; \Bigl\{\, x \;:\; x_i \in \bigl[u_i - \tfrac{h}{2},\, u_i + \tfrac{h}{2}\bigr] \text{ for every } i \,\Bigr\},$$
--
--   i.e. the preimage, under the canonical identification of the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$ with the space of functions $\{1,\dots,d\} \to \mathbb{R}$, of the product $\prod_{i} \bigl[u_i - \tfrac{h}{2}, u_i + \tfrac{h}{2}\bigr]$.
--
--   Presenting the cube as a product of intervals is what makes its measure-theoretic properties available: measurability, the volume $h^d$, and the product structure needed to compute integrals over cubes and over products of two cubes.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The identification is `WithLp.ofLp : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) → (Fin d → ℝ)`, which is the identity on underlying points and only changes the norm instance; the product set is `Set.pi Set.univ`. The hypothesis $h \ge 0$ is needed for the degenerate cases (for $h < 0$ the interval product is empty when $d \ge 1$ but is the whole one-point space when $d = 0$).
-- source:
